<p>Now run that bond signal through the Tax &amp; Wealth Desk’s mortgage renewal analysis. The 5-year fixed mortgage rate — quoted this morning at approximately 4.04% — is anchored directly to that 5-year bond yield. A yield that rose today on a day it should have fallen means the rate relief implied by a peace deal is not arriving as cleanly as the equity headlines suggested. The renewal conversation tomorrow is not simpler than it was this morning. It is harder, not easier, and the bond market just said so.</p>

<p>The connection the five desks together make visible is this: the Geopolitical Desk’s scenario 2 is the scenario the bond market priced today. That means the Economy Desk’s two-directional BoC dilemma did not resolve toward the cut side on Thursday despite everything that happened. Which means the Tax &amp; Wealth Desk’s mortgage renewal client is still in the same uncertain rate environment they were in this morning — and any advisor who told a client to wait for peace-driven rate relief needs to revisit that conversation before it becomes a missed renewal window.</p>', '', '', '[{"value":"-0.38%","label":"S&P 500 close"},{"value":"$100.06","label":"Brent crude close"},{"value":"3.27%","label":"Canada 5yr yield"},{"value":"$96.73","label":"Brent intraday low"}]', 'may07-behaviour.jpg', 'Photo: iStock.', 5, '2026-05-07', 'Daily Thread,Iran,Bond Yields,Mortgage Rates,Behavioural Finance,TSX,BoC', 0, 'CNBC (S&P 500 intraday data, May 7, 2026), Trading Economics (Brent crude, Canada 5-year bond yield), Fortune (oil price data), Investing.com (Canada bond yield historical data)');